  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSunday...The warming trend continues into Sunday as the upper- \nlevel ridge over the Southern Rockies sits in place. A ridge- \nriding trough is forecasted to dive into south-central Canada, \ndeepening a low pressure system and forcing a cold front down \ninto the Northern Plains. Southwesterly flow and low-level WAA \nintensify, allowing highs to soar into the low-to-mid-90s with \nheat indices potentially (40-70% chance) exceeding 100 degrees. \nAn Extreme Heat Watch has been issued for all of eastern Iowa, \nwest-central Illinois, and northwest Missouri for this heat\nthreat. By Sunday night, the cold front could reach \nnorthwestern Iowa as our cloud cover and PoPs increase \novernight. There is some ensemble signal of a MCS development \nacross northern Iowa that could move southeastward into our \nCWA, but uncertainty remains high with PoPs only sitting at \n30-50% north of Interstate 80. Despite this, SPC did issue a \nSlight Risk (Level 2 out of 5) for the Highway 20 corridor and a\nMarginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) for the rest of the CWA for \nthe potential (5-15% chance) for damaging winds with any \nthunderstorm complex that develops. The additional cloud cover \nshould limit lows to the mid-to-upper 70s, and once again, \nsetting up the potential for dangerous heat Monday ahead of the \ncold front.\n\nMonday...The shortwave trough continues to dive southeastward \ninto the Great Lakes region as the cold front inches towards our\nCWA by Monday afternoon. Dry air advection in the mid levels \nbehind Sunday night's convection is forecasted to suppress PoPs\n(10-20% coverage) along the actual surface front. With such \nwarm overnight lows, highs should easily reach the low-to- \nmid-90s again (70-100% chance), especially in southeast Iowa, \nwest-central Illinois, and northwest Missouri. Convergence \nalong the cold front could support moisture pooling with dew \npoints reaching the upper 70s ahead of the boundary. This will \nlead to localized maxima in heat indices throughout Monday \nafternoon with areas along and southwest of a line between the \nQuad Cities and Waterloo possibly seeing heat indices exceeding \n105 degrees (30-50% chance) and is why the Extreme Heat Watch \nextends through Monday evening as well. As the cold front clears\nthe area Monday night, northwesterly flow is expected to advect\nin slightly cooler, drier air as dew points drop back into the \nupper 60s and lower 70s.\n\nTuesday-Thursday...As another high pressure moves in and sits over\nIowa, conditions are likely (50-80%) to be quiet Tuesday into \nWednesday with mostly clear skies, highs in the upper 80s to low\n90s, and lows in the mid-60s to low 70s. Current LREF guidance\nhas the ridge over the Southern Rockies retrograding slowly to\nthe west throughout the middle of next week. This opens the door\nfor another shortwave to slide southeastward into the Upper\nMississippi River Valley on Thursday and introduces PoPs (10-20%\ncoverage) back into the forecast.",
